Exactly. Attention is an active filter, not a passive camera.

What you choose to notice changes the data that enters your model, which changes the story you build, which changes what you look for next. In control-theory terms, sensing and acting are coupled:

\text{attention} \rightarrow \text{observation} \rightarrow \text{model} \rightarrow \text{action} \rightarrow \text{new observation}

That makes “choosing what you notice” powerful, but also dangerous. It can become deliberate perception, or it can become a closed confirmation loop where you only collect evidence your current model already predicts.

So a strong Pantheon version would be: control salience, but audit omission. Choose your focal point deliberately, then periodically ask, “What would I have noticed if my current hypothesis were wrong?” That keeps attention as an instrument instead of letting it become a blindfold.